Layer</span></h2><h3><span class=3D"">Temporary Result (In-Memory, Within P=
rocess)</span></h3><ul><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=
=3D"">An array of </span><code>CapturedPacket</code><span class=3D""> struc=
tures in the ring buffer, each holding:</span></p><ul><li><p class=3D"ds-ma=
rkdown-paragraph"><code>Timestamp</code><span class=3D"">: QPC-converted na=
nosecond Unix epoch.</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph">=
<code>Length</code><span class=3D"">: Original wire length.</span></p></li>=
<li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><code>Interface_ID</code><span class=
=3D"">: Which adapter (in case of multi-homed capture).</span></p></li><li>=
<p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><code>Raw Packet Data</code><span class=
=3D"">: A contiguous byte array starting with the Ethernet header.</span></=
p></li></ul></li></ul><h3><span class=3D"">Final Result (Handover Artifact =
to Layer 2)</span></h3><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">=
The final, consumable output of Layer 1 is the </span><strong><span class=
=3D"">Named Shared Memory Segment</span></strong><span class=3D""> (</span>=
<code>Local\CognitiveFW_CaptureBuffer</code><span class=3D"">) and the </sp=
an><strong><span class=3D"">PacketReady Semaphore</span></strong><span clas=
s=3D"">.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">The =
Layer 2 =E2=80=93 Flow Assembly Engine (</span><code>FlowAssembler.exe</cod=
e><span class=3D"">) attaches to this same memory-mapped file and semaphore=
. The handover is a simple inter-process memory view. Layer 2 never calls a=
ny Npcap API. It simply waits on the semaphore and reads the packet descrip=
tors directly from the shared ring buffer, exactly where Layer 1 wrote them=
. This zero-copy IPC is the only way to handle 10 Gbps line rates on Window=
s without the kernel scheduler destroying performance.</span></p></div></di=
